<BaseLayout title="Trough Conveyors: Troughed Belt Idlers &#x26; Bulk Material Handling" description="Trough conveyors: troughing idler configurations, belt capacity calculations, bulk material handling for mining, aggregate, grain, and cement. CEMA standards and sizing." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-types/trough-conveyors/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema, articleSchema]}>
<h1>Trough Conveyors</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A trough conveyor shapes a flat belt into a concave trough using angled idler rollers, which roughly doubles to triples carrying capacity compared to a flat belt of the same width. The troughed profile contains bulk materials within the belt cross-section, preventing edge spillage on horizontal and inclined runs. Trough conveyors are the dominant bulk material handling technology for mining, aggregate, grain, cement, and power generation, over distances from short in-plant runs to overland systems many miles long.</p>
<h2 id="how-heading">How Troughing Works</h2>
<p>A standard belt conveyor runs flat, supporting material only on its horizontal surface. Material piles in a ridge whose height depends on the material's angle of repose. At the belt edges, material spills over. The solution is troughing: angled idler rollers push the belt edges upward, forming a U-shaped channel that contains material within the belt cross-section.</p>
<p>The carrying-side idlers (troughing idlers) consist of a center roller and two or more wing rollers mounted at an angle. The wing rollers lift the belt edges. The center roller supports the belt bottom. As the belt travels, it conforms to the idler shape, creating a continuous trough that moves with the belt. The return run uses flat rollers or V-return idlers because the belt carries no material on the return side.</p>
<p>Troughing angle is the single most important parameter. Steeper angles create deeper troughs with more cross-sectional area, but require more flexible belts. Standard angles are 20°, 35°, and 45°. The <a href="https://cemastore.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Conveyor Equipment Manufacturers Association (CEMA)</a> book <em>Belt Conveyors for Bulk Materials</em> is the reference for capacity and idler selection at each troughing angle and belt width. <a href="https://www.pooleyinc.com/wp-content/uploads/Belt-Capacity-Chart.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pooley's belt capacity chart</a> rates a flat belt at half the capacity of the same belt on 20° troughing idlers, and the 45° figures run about 45% higher than the 20° figures.</p>
<h2 id="idler-heading">Troughing Idler Types</h2>
<h3>Three-Roll Troughing Idlers</h3>
<p>The standard configuration uses one flat center roller and two angled wing rollers. Three-roll idlers handle the majority of bulk conveying applications. Rolls are equal-length in most designs, and unequal (longer center roll) troughers are used for picking belts. CEMA load classes, designated by letter (for example C, D, and E), rate idlers from lighter through heavier service. <a href="https://www.ppi-global.com/userdocs/literature/ppi/documents/idl_012_idler_selection_guide.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Precision Pulley &amp; Idler's selection guide</a> walks through choosing the class from belt width, troughing angle, and calculated idler load.</p>
<h3>Five-Roll Troughing Idlers</h3>
<p>Five-roll idlers use one center roller and four wing rollers (two per side at increasing angles) to create a deeper, more rounded trough. They suit very wide belts where three-roll idlers cannot achieve sufficient trough depth without excessive belt stress at the wing-to-center transition. Five-roll idlers distribute belt stress more evenly and reduce the belt's minimum required transverse flexibility.</p>
<h3>Impact Idlers</h3>
<p>Impact idlers mount at loading points where material drops onto the belt. They use rubber-cushioned rollers or rubber disc assemblies that absorb impact energy, protecting the belt from puncture and the idler bearings from shock loading. Impact idlers sit much closer together than carrying idlers. The PPI guide gives common spacing as one foot for impact idlers and 4 feet for carrying idlers, while noting spacing varies by design. Impact rating depends on lump weight and drop height, which PPI tabulates by drop height.</p>
<h3>Training (Self-Aligning) Idlers</h3>
<p>Training idlers pivot on a central swivel to correct belt mistracking. When the belt drifts to one side, the idler tilts, creating a corrective force that steers the belt back to center. Training idlers are installed at intervals along the carrying and return runs, with spacing set by conveyor length and tracking history. They supplement proper belt alignment but do not substitute for correct conveyor installation and tensioning.</p>
<h3>Return Idlers</h3>
<p>Return idlers support the belt on its return run (bottom side). Standard return idlers are flat single rollers or V-shaped two-roller sets. Rubber-disc return idlers clean carry-back material from the belt underside. Self-cleaning spiral return idlers use a helical rubber pattern that sheds sticky materials. Return idler spacing is wider than carrying idlers. PPI cites 10 feet as common, with some conveyors at 8 feet (double the carrying spacing) to simplify the framework.</p>
