<p>Capacities from <a href="https://www.pooleyinc.com/wp-content/uploads/Belt-Capacity-Chart.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Pooley's belt capacity chart</a>: tons per hour at 100 FPM belt speed, material at 100 lb per cubic foot, 20° surcharge angle, three equal-length rolls. Capacity scales linearly with belt speed and material density, within the maximum belt speed for the material and lump size. For other materials, the chart's formula is TPH = 0.03 x belt speed (FPM) x material weight (lb/ft³) x load cross section (ft²). See <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a> for pricing factors.</p>
<h2 id="belt-heading">Trough Conveyor Belt Types</h2>
<h3>Fabric-Reinforced Belts</h3>
<p>Multi-ply fabric belts use layers of polyester (EP), nylon (NN), or polyester-nylon (EP/NN) fabric for tensile reinforcement. Belt strength is rated in PIW (pounds per inch of width), and the required rating comes from the calculated belt tension. Fabric belts cover most in-plant and moderate-length conveyors. They cost less than steel-cord belts and are easier to splice in the field.</p>
<h3>Steel-Cord Reinforced Belts</h3>
<p>Steel-cord belts embed parallel steel cables in the belt carcass for extreme tensile strength. Their much higher tension ratings serve long-distance overland conveyors, high-tonnage <a href="/industries/mining-conveyors/">mining conveyors</a>, and applications where belt stretch must be minimized. They require vulcanized splicing with specialized equipment.</p>
<h3>Cover Grades</h3>
<p>The belt cover (top and bottom rubber layers) protects the reinforcement from abrasion, impact, heat, oil, and chemical attack. CEMA/RMA grades include: Grade I for severely abrasive conditions (mining, aggregate), Grade II for moderately abrasive conditions (grain, fertilizer, cement), and specialty grades for oil-resistant, fire-resistant, and heat-resistant applications. Cover thickness increases with impact and abrasion severity.</p>
<h3>Belt Flexibility</h3>
<p>Troughing requires the belt to flex transversely. Stiffer belts resist troughing and do not conform well to the idler profile, reducing effective carrying capacity and causing edge spillage. The belt must be flexible enough for the specified troughing angle. Higher troughing angles (45°) require more flexible belts than lower angles (20°). Belt manufacturers publish minimum recommended troughing angles for each belt construction.</p>
<h2 id="apps-heading">Trough Conveyor Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Mining and aggregate:</strong> Trough conveyors move ore, overburden, crushed stone, sand, and gravel from extraction points to processing plants. Wide belts handle throughputs from hundreds to thousands of tons per hour. Overland conveyors replace haul trucks on long, fixed routes, reducing fuel, tire, and road maintenance costs.</p>
<p><strong>Grain and agriculture:</strong> Troughed belts transport grain from receiving pits to storage silos and from silos to processing or loadout. The enclosed trough reduces grain loss from wind and edge spillage. Food-grade belt covers prevent contamination. Inclined trough conveyors use <a href="/conveyor-types/cleated-conveyors/">cleated belts</a> or chevron-patterned covers for steep angles.</p>
<p><strong>Cement and concrete:</strong> Trough conveyors move limestone, clinker, sand, and finished cement from quarry to kiln to silo to loadout. High-temperature belt covers handle hot clinker from the kiln. Dust-control hoods and enclosed galleries minimize fugitive dust emissions.</p>
<p><strong>Power generation:</strong> Coal-fired power plants use trough conveyors to transport coal from rail unloading facilities or barge docks to boiler feed silos. Fire-resistant belt covers and fire detection are standard practice for coal handling. Confirm requirements with the plant's insurer and the authority having jurisdiction.</p>
<p><strong>Packaging bulk materials:</strong> In <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging line</a> applications, narrower trough conveyors transport bulk ingredients from storage to filling machines. Sugar, salt, flour, pet food, fertilizer, and chemical powders move through troughed belt systems that feed weighing and bagging equipment.</p>
<p><strong>Waste and recycling:</strong> Trough conveyors transport municipal solid waste, construction debris, and recyclables from tipping floors to sorting stations.Wide belts handle the variable density and size of waste streams. Impact idlers at loading points absorb the shock of heavy items dropped from loaders.</p>
<h2 id="ploughs">Belt Ploughs (Plows) on Trough Conveyors</h2>
<p>A conveyor plough is a blade set diagonally across the belt to push material off the side. Ploughs come in two main uses on troughed belt conveyors.</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Plough Type</th>
<th>Where It Mounts</th>
<th>What It Does</th>
<th>Key Constraint</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Discharge plough (single-sided or V)</td>
<td>Carrying side, at a fixed discharge point</td>
<td>Diverts material off one or both belt edges into bins, hoppers, or chutes</td>
<td>Belt must be flattened at the plough, and discharge points are fixed</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Return-side V-plough</td>
<td>Inside of the return belt, ahead of the tail pulley</td>
<td>Clears rocks and carryback before they reach the tail pulley</td>
<td>Protects pulley, lagging, and splices, but does not replace primary and secondary cleaners</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p><a href="https://feeco.com/belt-trippers-vs-plows/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FEECO notes</a> that discharge ploughs need the belt to pass through a flattening structure so the blade can clear it, that the discharge locations are locked in place, and that ploughs remove the troughed sidewall protection, which causes faster wear on the belt and blade with large or abrasive material. A lift bed or slider section at the plough supports the flattened belt. Ploughs work best on free-flowing material and for filling multiple storage hoppers. For movable or many discharge points, a belt tripper is the usual alternative.</p>
<p>When specifying a discharge plough, give the supplier the material, lump size, belt width and speed, troughing angle, the number and location of discharge points, and whether each plough must raise and lower under control.</p>
<h2 id="design-heading">Design Considerations</h2>
<h3>Loading &amp; Transfer Points</h3>
<p>Material loading is the highest-stress point on a trough conveyor. Proper chute design directs material onto the belt center at a velocity matching the belt speed. Impact idlers absorb drop energy. Skirt boards contain material during the settling zone. Poor loading causes belt mistracking, premature wear, and fugitive material spillage. CEMA's <em>Belt Conveyors for Bulk Materials</em> provides loading point engineering guidance.</p>
<h3>Belt Cleaning</h3>
<p>Material carry-back on the belt return side causes buildup on return idlers, tracking problems, and housekeeping issues. Primary belt cleaners mount at the head pulley discharge point. Secondary cleaners mount behind the head pulley. Return-side plows, such as the <a href="https://www.flexco.com/EN/Product-Series/Belt-Cleaning-Systems/V-Plow.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Flexco V-Plow</a>, clean the inside of the return belt ahead of the tail pulley so rocks and carryback do not damage the pulley, lagging, or splices. Effective belt cleaning is especially critical for sticky materials like wet clay, damp coal, and processed foods.</p>
<h3>Conveyor Structure</h3>
<p>Trough conveyor support structures include ground-level stringers, elevated gallery frames, and pipe trusses. Structural design must account for belt tension, material weight, wind loading (for outdoor installations), seismic requirements, and maintenance access. Walkways and access platforms along the conveyor length are required for idler replacement, belt inspection, and safety per MSHA and OSHA standards.</p>
<h3>Drive Systems</h3>
<p>Trough conveyors use head-end, tail-end, or dual-pulley drive configurations depending on conveyor length and load. Short conveyors typically use a single head-end drive. Long conveyors use dual drives or intermediate booster drives. <a href="/resources/conveyor-motors-drives/">Variable frequency drives</a> (VFDs) enable soft starting, speed control, and energy savings at partial loads. Regenerative braking recovers energy on decline conveyors carrying heavy loads downhill.</p>
